                <pb facs="0627" n="493" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg493"/>

                <head>No. 349.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. McLane.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-10-20"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 20,
                        1887</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 268.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my instruction to your
                    legation, No. 217, of the 16th of April last, relative to obtaining the
                    discharge from the French army of Mr. John Fruchier, a naturalized American
                    citizen of French birth I will thank you to inform me as to what progress has
                    been made in the matter, in order that the Department may be able to answer the
                    recent inquiry of his excellency the governor of Nevada relative to the
                    subject.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
